<p>Writers have the blank page. Designers have the infinite canvas. Both are terrifying because they offer no resistance. A good constraint is not a limitation; it is a shape to push against.</p>

<p>The constraints I remember most fondly are almost never the ones I would have chosen. A single typeface. A two-week deadline. A feature that had to fit in one screen. A budget that ruled out the obvious solution. Each one closed a door and made the remaining rooms feel more interesting.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Freedom is not the absence of limits. It is the presence of a few limits you respect.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The trick is to choose constraints that clarify rather than punish. “No JavaScript” clarifies. “No dark mode, ever” punishes. A useful constraint removes a category of decisions, so you can spend your attention on the decisions that remain.</p>

<p>Lately mine have been simple: one page, one column, one typeface, one clear action per screen. Most of the time that is enough. When it isn’t, the constraint tells me exactly where to look.</p>
